#!/bin/sh

qemu-system-i386 -icount shift=7 -hda hda.img -nic user,ipv6=off,model=ne2k_pci -device sb16 -vga cirrus -snapshot -no-shutdown -display gtk,zoom-to-fit=on

# Why -icount shift=7?
#
# When networking starts, Windows for Workgroups 3.11's NDIS.386 driver
# measures the CPU speed. It runs 0x100000 (1,048,576) LOOP instructions
# and times them with the VMM's Get_System_Time service, which counts in
# milliseconds. It then divides by the elapsed time. If the loop takes
# less than 1 ms, the elapsed time is 0, the divide faults, and Windows
# hangs on a black screen right after the logo. This is the same "fast
# CPU" bug that hit Windows 95 on fast AMD K6 machines.
#
# Since QEMU 7.2 (commit 2255da49, "target/i386: Use gen_jmp_rel for
# loop, repz, jecxz insns"), TCG chains LOOP straight to the next
# translated block instead of returning to the main loop on every
# iteration. On a modern host the 1M iterations then finish in well
# under a millisecond. Whether it fails depends on host load, which is
# why some people could boot and others could not.
#
# -icount shift=7 decouples guest time from host time: each guest
# instruction advances the virtual clock by 2^7 = 128 ns, so the guest
# runs at a fixed ~8 MIPS (roughly a fast 486) whatever the host speed.
# The calibration loop then always takes ~134 ms of guest time and the
# divide never sees 0. Lower shift values are faster but leave less
# margin; shift=auto adapts to the host and would bring the bug back.
#
# This (finally!) fixes https://github.com/rtts/win311/issues/2
